From bbd8325366317f5c95ec0bb25ebf345cc4d3c50a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ashish Kumar Singh <ashishkmr472@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 14:50:43 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] [XLA:CPU] A deserialized thunk sequence keeps one resource
 across its nested sequences

The CPU thunk emitter gives every collective of a program one communicator resource, those in a while loop's body,
a call's and a conditional's branches included, and a while, call or conditional thunk uses its nested sequences'
resources, so the execution graph orders a loop against the collectives beside it. ThunkSequenceSerDesProtobuf
deserialized each nested sequence's resources apart (WhileThunkFromProto, CallThunkFromProto and
ConditionalThunkFromProto each built a fresh serdes, and the resources the enclosing sequence listed for the nested
thunk went unused): after a roundtrip a loop no longer shared its body's communicator with the collectives beside
it, the graph lost that edge, and ranks entered the loop's all-reduce and an all-gather beside the loop in different
orders, their rendezvous timing out.

A serialized resource now carries an id unique across the sequence tree (ResourceUsersProto.resource_id), and one
id-to-resource map is threaded through the nested sequences' serdes, so a resource shared across them is
deserialized once. Resources of different ids stay apart (two communicators of one kind are two resources), and
each top-level ToProto or FromProto call has its own map. A proto without ids (resource_id 0) deserializes as before.
